WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.900
BitsAndBytes.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:07.000
The fastest path from a checkpoint on the Hub to a model running on a small GPU.

3
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.433
BitsAndBytes quantizes at load time.

4
00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:13.733
You start with any ordinary fp16 checkpoint.

5
00:00:14.033 --> 00:00:16.100
You pass a single config object.

6
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.233
The weights are compressed on the fly as they stream in.

7
00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:22.700
No pre-quantized file, and no calibration pass.

8
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:25.167
Three settings matter.

9
00:00:25.467 --> 00:00:31.200
NF4 is a four bit grid whose bins follow the normal distribution real weights have.

10
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:38.400
The compute dtype means matmuls dequantize to bf16, so storage is small but math stays wide.

11
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:44.233
Double quantization compresses the scale factors too, saving about point four bits per weight.

12
00:00:45.433 --> 00:00:47.400
How does it compare to the other methods?

13
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.854
BitsAndBytes quantizes at load time.

14
00:00:50.854 --> 00:00:53.833
GPTQ and AWQ are prepared offline.

15
00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:58.333
BitsAndBytes gives maximum convenience, but slightly slower inference.

16
00:00:58.633 --> 00:01:02.300
So it's ideal for experiments, less so for hot serving paths.

17
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:04.700
Here is the config.

18
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.733
Turn on four bit loading with the NF4 type.

19
00:01:08.033 --> 00:01:10.733
Set bf16 as the compute dtype.

20
00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:12.667
Turn on double quantization.

21
00:01:12.967 --> 00:01:16.867
The model now takes about four gigabytes, down from fourteen and a half.

22
00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:21.100
Say you're choosing the answering model for Mission Control.

23
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:26.967
You want to audition five seven billion candidates, but only two have pre-quantized files.

24
00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:29.477
BitsAndBytes removes the blocker.

25
00:01:29.477 --> 00:01:34.700
Every checkpoint loads in four bit, and all five fit your twelve gigabyte GPU.

26
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.100
Then graduate the winner to AWQ for serving.

27
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.133
Let's recap.

28
00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:42.633
BitsAndBytes quantizes at load time.

29
00:01:42.933 --> 00:01:46.200
NF4 for storage, bf16 for compute.

30
00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.900
Double quantization saves a bit more.

31
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.590
Use it for experiments, then graduate to AWQ.

32
00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:54.700
Next up: prompt engineering.
